294 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Warehouse inventory accuracy relies on frequent and comprehensive rack scanning. The traditional approach involves human workers walking aisles with handheld scanners, a process that is time-consuming, prone to errors due to fatigue, and difficult to scale as facilities expand.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The gap: an affordable, truly autonomous platform that can operate reliably in real warehouse conditions without constant supervision or specialized infrastructure.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Existing Solutions)
/Alt ()
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Warehouse inventory management is critical for operational efficiency, yet manual scanning is slow and error-prone while automated solutions require expensive mechanical systems for multi-level shelf access. Our approach uses a lightweight double-pulley mechanism with sensor feedback and custom cable management to achieve reliable vertical scanning at significantly lower cost while maintaining precise alignment and seamless navigation integration.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Existing commercial and research platforms partially address warehouse mobility but do not provide a complete workflow for structured vertical scanning.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
