645 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The electronics architecture follows a hierarchical design separating high-level deliberation from real-time control. The Raspberry Pi 4 runs ROS2 nodes for navigation, SLAM, and mission planning, while the Raspberry Pi Pico 2 handles deterministic motor control and sensor interfacing. This separation ensures real-time guarantees for low-level control while allowing the Pi 4 to handle computationally intensive tasks without timing constraints.)
/E (The electronics architecture follows a hierarchical design separating high-level deliberation from real-time control. The Raspberry Pi 4 runs ROS2 nodes for navigation, SLAM, and mission planning, while the Raspberry Pi Pico 2 handles deterministic motor control and sensor interfacing. This separation ensures real-time guarantees for low-level control while allowing the Pi 4 to handle computationally intensive tasks without timing constraints.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [37]
>>
endobj
646 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Communication between the two boards occurs via USB serial at 115200 baud. The Pi 4 sends velocity commands and receives odometry data, while the Pico 2 translates these commands into PWM signals for motor drivers and aggregates encoder readings.)
/E (Communication between the two boards occurs via USB serial at 115200 baud. The Pi 4 sends velocity commands and receives odometry data, while the Pico 2 translates these commands into PWM signals for motor drivers and aggregates encoder readings.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [38]
>>
endobj
