1871 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Assuming the total weight of the robot to come down to a maximum of 16kg \(More than our current weight\).)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1872 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Load per Wheel = Total Mass/4 = 16kg/4 = 4 kg)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1873 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (We assumed the warehouse floor to have a coefficient of friction of 1.1 since the wheels are made of rubber and a standard warehouse floor is made of concrete.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1874 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Tractive Force per Wheel = Masswheel x CoF = 4kg x 1.1 = 4.4 kgf)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1875 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (We are using wheels with their wheel size = 10 cm)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1876 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Stall Torque is the maximum amount of rotational force \(torque\) a motor can produce when its output shaft is completely stopped \(stalled\) while receiving full power. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [7 8]
>>
endobj
1877 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Minimum Stall Torque = Forcetractive x \(Wheel Radius\) = 4.4kgf x 5cm = 22kgf-cm)
/Pg 42 0 R
>>
endobj
1878 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The motor we finally selected has a stall torque of 27.18kgf-cm which is well over the calculated value of 22kgf-cm, ensuring the smooth functionality of the motors.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1879 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Pulley Motor Calculations)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
1880 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The lower pulley will have to take a load of 1.5kgs up 1.8m in order to be able to scan all the inventory in less than 3min, we estimated that the motor will have to move up in just around 18 seconds. The outer diameter of the pulley is 16mm.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1881 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Speed = 1.8/18 = 0.1m/s)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1882 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <FEFF0052006F0074006100740069006F006E0061006C0020005300700065006500640020003D00200030002E0031002F002803C00020007800200030002E00300031003600290020003D002000310032003000720070006D>
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1883 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Since the speed was the dictating factor and the torque required to lift a 1.5kg mass to a height of 2m is 0.118Nm = 1.18 kg-cm, we had the liberty to pick any motor we wanted. We selected a motor with a 12.5kg-cm torque. This creates a good safety margin which ensures that the motor turns steadily even under friction, inefficiencies, or drag in the system.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1884 0 obj
